steps.body:
Joining values with a separator is the classic builder job, and the classic bug is a trailing `, ` at the end. `String.join` handles a `String[]`, but when you filter values or join numbers you build it yourself. Two common fixes:

**Check first.** Before each value, append the separator only if something is already there:

```java
if (sb.length() > 0) {
    sb.append(", ");
}
sb.append(n);
```

**Trim after.** Append the separator after every value, then cut the last one off with `setLength`, which keeps only the first n chars:

```java
if (sb.length() > 0) {
    sb.setLength(sb.length() - 2); // ", " is 2 chars
}
```

Guard the trim: if nothing was appended, `setLength(-2)` throws. The `length() > 0` check only works when the builder starts empty, so add labels like `Evens: ` outside the builder. `setLength(0)` clears a builder so you can reuse it.

example.code:
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] nums = {3, 8, 12, 5, 6, 7, 10};

        StringBuilder checked = new StringBuilder();
        for (int n : nums) {
            if (n % 2 == 0) {
                if (checked.length() > 0) {
                    checked.append(", ");
                }
                checked.append(n);
            }
        }
        System.out.println("Evens: " + checked);

        StringBuilder trimmed = new StringBuilder();
        for (int n : nums) {
            if (n % 2 == 0) {
                trimmed.append(n).append(", ");
            }
        }
        if (trimmed.length() > 0) {
            trimmed.setLength(trimmed.length() - 2);
        }
        System.out.println("Evens: " + trimmed);
    }
}

